  "slides.1.highlights.0.title": "Ontario",
  "slides.1.highlights.0.content": "Three recognised areas: Niagara Peninsula, Lake Erie North Shore and Prince Edward County (2007, most northerly, no sub-appellations); Niagara has 3 regional appellations and 10 sub-appellations (3 + 4 + 3), and the escarpment's three benches are Short Hills, Twenty Mile and Beamsville; Lake Erie North Shore's sub-appellation is South Islands; PEC has limestone and buries its vines",
  "slides.1.highlights.1.title": "British Columbia",
  "slides.1.highlights.1.content": "Nine GIs and 12 sub-GIs (11 in the Okanagan plus Cowichan Valley); Okanagan GI in 1990; Golden Mile Bench was the first sub-GI (2015); six Okanagan sub-GIs added in 2022; Cowichan on 10 July 2020, the first outside the Okanagan; a GI label, sub-GIs included, needs at least 85% from the place (current regulation); Similkameen is the organic capital with no sub-GIs",
  "slides.1.highlights.2.title": "The east",
  "slides.1.highlights.2.content": "Nova Scotia: Tidal Bay from June 2012, 100% Nova Scotia grapes, no more than 11% alcohol, annual blind-panel approval; L'Acadie Blanc is the leading white. Quebec: cold-hardy hybrids (such as Frontenac), under 10% vinifera, Vin du Québec PGI guaranteeing 100% Quebec grapes since 2018, and the SAQ monopoly",
